# Basic PCB Design Project

This folder contains a basic PCB design project built around an ESP32-WROOM-32UE module. The board combines sensing, display, memory, USB-C power/input, and supporting power regulation components into a compact embedded IoT hardware design.

## Project Overview

The PCB is designed as a learning and prototyping project for embedded hardware development. It demonstrates how to create a schematic, select components, prepare a bill of materials, and generate PCB layout views for fabrication or review.

Main features include:

- ESP32-WROOM-32UE Wi-Fi/Bluetooth module
- 0.96 inch SSD1306 OLED display interface
- DHT22 temperature and humidity sensor
- MQ9 gas sensor interface
- LDR light sensing input
- AT24C02 EEPROM memory
- USB Type-C connector
- AMS1117 3.3V voltage regulator
- Supporting capacitors and resistors

## Folder Contents

| File | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `BOM_PCB.csv` | Bill of materials with component names, designators, footprints, quantities, and supplier details. |
| `Schematic of PCB` | Circuit schematic for the PCB design. |
| `PCB_2D view.png` | 2D preview of the completed PCB layout. |
| `PCB_3D View.png` | 3D preview of the PCB with components placed. |
| `PCB_Design_frontview.png` | Top copper/layer view of the PCB design. |
| `PCB_Design_Backview.png` | Bottom copper/layer view of the PCB design. |

## Key Components

| Component | Designator | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ESP32-WROOM-32UE | U1 | Main microcontroller with Wi-Fi and Bluetooth support. |
| AMS1117-3.3V | U2 | 3.3V voltage regulation for the ESP32 and peripherals. |
| DHT22 | U3 | Temperature and humidity sensing. |
| MQ9 Gas Sensor | U4 | Gas detection input. |
| AT24C02 EEPROM | U5 | External non-volatile memory. |
| SSD1306 OLED | LCD1 | Local display output. |
| USB Type-C | USB1 | Power/input connector. |
| LDR | R2 | Light intensity sensing through ADC. |

## Design Notes

- Verify all footprints before sending the PCB for fabrication.
- Check the ESP32 power supply requirements and ensure the 3.3V regulator can provide enough current.
- Confirm sensor pin mappings with the firmware before assembly.
- Review top and bottom layer routing for shorts, clearance issues, and manufacturability.
- Use the BOM file when ordering parts from suppliers such as LCSC, eBay, or SeeedStudio.

## Suggested Workflow

1. Review the schematic.
2. Check all component footprints against real part dimensions.
3. Inspect the top and bottom PCB layer images.
4. Review the 2D and 3D board views.
5. Validate the BOM before purchasing components.
6. Run design rule checks in the PCB design tool before fabrication.

## Applications

This PCB design can be used as a foundation for beginner embedded IoT projects such as environmental monitoring, air quality sensing, light-based automation, and ESP32 sensor dashboards.
